{"kind":"assert","text":"Assessment of the previous-snapshot availability condition of add29762 (#20008), against main 1fba3d55 and head 61b33f3785d14e0acfcc2f357f619256dceb93a0. Where a previous snapshot exists: only in-process, as [snapshotCache](internal/app/app.go@1fba3d550091f720e48521bb89f222bd4c218af8:2500) under [snapshotWithSource](internal/app/app.go@1fba3d550091f720e48521bb89f222bd4c218af8:2490), which holds snapshotMu for the whole head read, audit and fold, and is entered only through the single [flight](internal/app/app.go@1fba3d550091f720e48521bb89f222bd4c218af8:2457) that every reader joins ([SnapshotWithSource](internal/app/app.go@1fba3d550091f720e48521bb89f222bd4c218af8:2438)). After a resident restart there is no previous snapshot at all; the checkpoint is what the rebuild verifies. So the only candidate scenario is a cold re-audit with a same-profile cache already in memory, which happens when the incremental continuation cannot be used (a rewind or a discarded reader). What the current contracts say about serving it: the resident status is anchored to one exact frontier (service.Status.Cursor.Frontier, statusFromLive at [server.go](internal/service/server.go@1fba3d550091f720e48521bb89f222bd4c218af8:201)); the MCP adapter refuses a resident answer whose frontier is not the local head (currentResidentOrientation, cmd/gitseq-mcp/main.go); gs status validates the remote frontier against the local ref and falls back locally on mismatch (validateRemoteFrontier, cmd/gs/main.go); the whoami page landed today says the answer is anchored to an exact durable frontier; and admission judges bases only against the current verified snapshot. An old-frontier answer marked rebuilding would therefore be refused or discarded by every current consumer, and making one of them accept it is a new interpretation of what status means, not a mechanical exposure. Conclusion: under current contracts there is no safe previous-snapshot scenario to expose without an adopted decision; the honest behaviour, already delivered and unchanged here, is that readers join the single flight and the rebuild progress endpoint reports progress, and the head clock added at 61b33f3785d14e0acfcc2f357f619256dceb93a0 never blocks on that flight. I am not implementing a qualified old-frontier read surface under this request. Requested disposition: planner, please either ratify this assert as the scope disposition that condition 2 is discharged by assessment, or file a proposal for a qualified previous-frontier read surface for ordinary adoption, after which its implementation would be a separate assignment.","body":{"condition":"previous-snapshot availability","head":"61b33f3785d14e0acfcc2f357f619256dceb93a0","request":"git:sha1:5d2622748872b7e2dec3fe5c59e4be73a35e0bc8#git:sha1:add297623a7c5f1d22725d891def3bcfe13ab87a","stale_bases":"git:sha1:5d2622748872b7e2dec3fe5c59e4be73a35e0bc8#git:sha1:2d521563cf60b09d287f04e71667ceae931d4356, git:sha1:5d2622748872b7e2dec3fe5c59e4be73a35e0bc8#git:sha1:8f533029945dcd37f625d79e47918b0922cc6d85 stale; retired bases: git:sha1:5d2622748872b7e2dec3fe5c59e4be73a35e0bc8#git:sha1:001b2e3b06105d22946682cb90201bc66c1c6a26, git:sha1:5d2622748872b7e2dec3fe5c59e4be73a35e0bc8#git:sha1:03270b167c8f14b55a31919b2489715ee513b20f, git:sha1:5d2622748872b7e2dec3fe5c59e4be73a35e0bc8#git:sha1:07e8e48166ea90a1d864c829f36902817926f6e7, git:sha1:5d2622748872b7e2dec3fe5c59e4be73a35e0bc8#git:sha1:0a6f748b48ce807b2efbe9f4c8c245dda4d90359 and 122 more"}}